Date: Sat, 11 Sep 1993 11:15:18 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: Why I hate namedropping

T. David Burns says:
> My real field, Economics, is still pretty much dominated by Popperian
> thought, or even unreconstructed pre-Popperian Positivism (try saying that
> fast three times). There are of course some post-modernists at the margin.
> 
> My observation on this list leads me to believe that the other sciences (or
> at least CS?) also continue to dance to the Popper song. Is that so? Is
> there an Extropian epistemology? What about the evolutionary
> epistemologists?
> 
> I guess I could check the latest rev of the Extropian principles in the
> latest Extropy. What do YOU think?

I think its all meaningless name slinging.

Some things I believe resemble things Popper has said. Some things
resemble things Kuhn has said. Some things resemble things RAW has
said. Some things resemble things Marx has said, for crissake (I
certainly read enough of his stuff, and not every line was drivel.)

What matters is not who said something but what the ideas are. I
really resent the trend some pepole have on this list to say things
like "Ooh, thats just silly stuff Anton Quux said in 1530, and we all
know that no serious scholars took him seriously", or "Ooh, thats just
silly fooanism, and we know thats garbage".

Ideas must be judged in and of themselves. Its certainly fine to use
ARGUMENTS others have proposed that you remember were used in the past
to attack similar ideas, but once you start playing the "I remember
more philosophers names than you do" game you lose. You can't argue by
invoking the names of philosophers. There is no substitute for using
the actual arguments.

I also hate pointing fingers, so I won't name the major offenders, but
a couple of them are people I'm quite fond of, and its a shame to see
you guys operating entirely in ad hominem mode. 

Perry

