Date: Sat, 11 Sep 1993 12:56:16 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: Discovery, Concepts, and Rights 

starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu says:
> >From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
> >Sounds very much like the notion that evolution can't occur, Tim.  You
> >can invent things without concepts -- but until you invent concepts
> >you can't conceptualize what you've invented. Nature, unconsciously,
> >does lots of inventing -- look at the invention of photosynthesis, for
> >example -- a great invention, but certainly not one requiring the
> >existance of consciousness, let alone concepts.
> 
> You abuse the term "invention," which entails intentional causation of
> necessity.  Things can be formed without concepts, but not literally made.

In other words, Tim, you've just defined the problem out of existance.

Sorry, you don't get off that easily.

> We can form things without concepts, unconsciously, as occurs naturally all
> the time, but these are not inventions, not "made" by anyone.

So, you are telling me that when a mentally retarded and autistic girl
produces stunning drawings of wild horses (such a case exists), she
hasn't drawn them, they've simply come into being. Bull.

Nature doesn't consciously invent things, but it does innovate and
develop things. Photosynthesis in a plant is if anything orders of
magnitude more complicated than a steel mill -- and yet you are
unwilling to grant it the same status.

You who are supposedly a champion of markets should understand the
importance of spontaneous orders.

The human invention we call "rights" is a case of spontaneous order.
Just as with photosynthesis, it isn't something you will find in the
structure of the universe, nor is it a necessity. 

> >You're the one suffering from a fallacy here -- the fallacy
> >that what you are doing is formal reasoning, when in fact you can't do
> >formal reasoning on anything other than a formal system.
> 
> I've thought about this charge of your since you last made it
> against me, and I've come to the conclusion that you're mistaken in
> placing formal reasoning as primary to informal.  Formal reasoning
> is reasoning stripped of referents, of intentional relation to
> concrete objects.  It is a derivative form of reasoning, not a
> primary.  The primary form of reasoning is intentional, referential,
> in relation to concrete objects.

You need a course in logic, Tim. Logic doesn't ever refer to the real
world -- it can't. The real world doesn't operate according to nice
logical categories. Logic can tie itself into knots that don't exist
in the real world, like the barber's paradox. Have a look right here
at an example of it. (You never go down to concrete examples, so I
will.)

1) John shaves all the men in town who do not shave themselves.
2) Who shaves John?

Answer that question, Tim, with a rigorous logical answer, and I'll
believe everything you say about logic. You have to come up with an
answer that doesn't resort to the messy "real world" though. The real
answer is, of course, that you can't define who shaves whom with a
nice simple english sentence, but trying to believe in Randroid myths
about the power of deductive reasoning leads you into traps like this.

Informal reasoning leads you down garden paths. English lets you state
concepts that have no applicability to reality. You can then pretend
to reason on them, making undetectable failures along the way, and
come to insane conclusions. I can "prove" to you lots of things, most
of which are bullshit, just by making slight mistakes in phrasing and
then going on from them.

Mathematical logic deals very well with abstract mathematical objects.
It does o.k. with things like the laws of physics. It works weakly
when you get down to things like sociology.

> You like to skip over the questions that arise from the internal, first-
> person point of view, but I think this is a mistake.  Most recently, a form
> of genetic determinism has emerged as the answer given by you and Dave
> Krieger to the question of why we pick the goals we do, among other things.
> 
> How do you know?

I form a hypothesis and test it against the world until it is
falsified. Thus far it hs held up and thus I have a reasonable amount
of confidence in it.

> This is yet another commission of the fallacy of self-exclusion, however.
> If your preferences are the result of genetic determinism, then your claim
> that your preferences are the result of genetic determinism is a result of
> genetic determinism, and may or may not be true.  This is neither falsifiable
> nor axiomatic, but a mystical article of faith.

I don't see the reasoning here. Its another case of your trying to use
english as a set of inputs to the machinery of logic. 

1) The mere fact that goals are the result of genetic conditioning
   doesn't mean that we are incapable of reasoning, even if our reason
   is also the result of genetic conditioning.
2) Even were we incapable of reasoning and simply blurted out one idea
   or another because of our genes, this is insufficient reason to
   claim we are wrong -- you must still independantly falsify the
   hypothesis. Even a broken clock is right twice a day, and not
   everything an idiot says is wrong. All you are doing is extending
   ad hominem attack -- you cannot validly attack an argument because
   of its messenger.

> You seem to think that I'm denying the role of custom in determining rights,
> but that's not it at all.  I'm trying to address the fact that customs are
> not irreducible primaries, but have their origin in our nature.

Thats fine, as far as it goes, but you miss an important point...

> >Contract law as we know it is very much a human creation
> 
> It is not any kind of creation.  To use a term you may be more familiar
> with, it is an emergent property.

You are arguing semantics here. I can call it a "Foosbian blork" if
you like. Use any words you like. The point is that many different
kinds of rules for contracts have arisen, some radically conflicting
with each other, in different places. Many different kinds of family
law, too -- as I've mentioned, in ancient Rome you could kill your 30
year old child without consequence as he remained your property until
you freed him or died. We can argue for or against particular
mechanisms, but most of the mechanisms of what we call "natural law"
are nothing more than custom and predjudice. 

Indeed, what we consider to be proper is disliked by the vast bulk of
our neighbors, and vice versa. 

Perry

