  I agree totally with Perry, although I do think it can be said that our
senses come to approximate the current reality over time. Put a person
into a VR sufficiently complex enough to perform science in and the
VRnaut will eventually come to determine the "laws" of that reality.
This is a testament to the human brain's ability to correlate data.

Steve Jobs made an excellent point about video games in that they are
great learning tools. Take Pong, for example. The primary thing to be learned
is the law behind the deflection angle and velocity in the game. Now
look at galactic bloodshed, stock market games, flight simulators, etc.
The human playing quickly learns very complex principles without ever
seeing an equation. He thinks this will be the wave of the future in
education. (e.g. we will learn about science/math/economics/etc by
